---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';

const headings = [
  { text: "Overview", slug: "overview", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Words and Phrases", slug: "words-and-phrases", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Field Clauses", slug: "field-clauses", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Negation and Missing Fields", slug: "negation", depth: 2 },
  { text: "OR Groups", slug: "or-groups", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Built-in Fields", slug: "built-in-fields", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Metadata Fields", slug: "metadata-fields", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Time and Scope", slug: "time-and-scope", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Examples", slug: "examples", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Errors", slug: "errors", depth: 2 },
  { text: "API", slug: "api", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Limits", slug: "limits", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Storage Engines", slug: "storage-engines", depth: 2 }
];

const keywords = [
  "LogTide query syntax",
  "log search query language",
  "log query field filters",
  "search logs by metadata",
  "log search OR exclude phrase",
  "LogTide logs API q parameter"
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Query Syntax"
  description="The LogTide log query language: words, quoted phrases, field filters, negation, OR groups, metadata exists and in clauses, limits, and how search behaves on TimescaleDB, ClickHouse and MongoDB."
  keywords={keywords}
  headings={headings}
>
  <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
    <div class="p-2 bg-primary/10 rounded-lg">
      <Icon name="lucide:search" class="w-6 h-6 text-primary-readable" />
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold">Query Syntax</h1>
  </div>

  <p class="text-lg text-muted-foreground mb-8">
    One search language for the logs explorer, live tail, saved searches, export and threshold
    alert rules. It is the same on TimescaleDB, ClickHouse and MongoDB, so a query you write once
    returns the same logs on every storage engine.
  </p>

  <h2 id="overview" class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border pb-2">
    Overview
  </h2>

  <div class="mb-8 space-y-4">
    <p>
      A query is a list of clauses separated by whitespace. Every clause must match, so
      <code>timeout db</code> finds logs containing both words. Clauses are either free text
      (matched against the log message) or <code>key:value</code> field filters.
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-border text-left">
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Syntax</th>
            <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Meaning</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-muted-foreground">
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>timeout</code></td><td class="py-2">The message contains the word <code>timeout</code> (case-insensitive).</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>"connection refused"</code></td><td class="py-2">The message contains this exact phrase (case-insensitive).</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>-debug</code> &middot; <code>-"health check"</code></td><td class="py-2">Exclude logs matching the word or phrase.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>timeout OR refused</code></td><td class="py-2">Either word. <code>OR</code> must be uppercase.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>service:api</code></td><td class="py-2">Field equals value.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>-service:api</code></td><td class="py-2">Field does not equal value.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>level:(error OR critical)</code></td><td class="py-2">Field is one of the listed values.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>-level:(debug OR info)</code></td><td class="py-2">Field is none of the listed values.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>user.id:*</code></td><td class="py-2">Metadata key exists.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>-user.id:*</code></td><td class="py-2">Metadata key does not exist.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>path:*admin*</code></td><td class="py-2">Metadata value contains <code>admin</code> (case-insensitive).</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>error.message:"disk full"</code></td><td class="py-2">Quote a field value that contains spaces: <code>key:"value with spaces"</code>.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      The logs explorer highlights the query as you type, underlines the offending token when
      something is wrong, and autocompletes field names and values. An explicit <code>AND</code>
      between two clauses is accepted and means the same as whitespace.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="words-and-phrases" class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border pb-2">
    Words and Phrases
  </h2>

  <div class="mb-8 space-y-4">
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 ml-2">
      <li><strong>Word</strong> - a run of letters, digits and underscores, such as <code>timeout</code> or <code>user_id</code>. It matches whole words in the message, ignoring case. <code>time</code> does not match <code>timeout</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Phrase</strong> - text in double quotes. It matches anywhere in the message as a case-insensitive substring. Use <code>\"</code> for a quote and <code>\\</code> for a backslash inside a phrase.</li>
      <li><strong>Anything else is a phrase too.</strong> A term containing punctuation, such as <code>10:42:13</code>, <code>http://example.com/health</code>, <code>10.0.0.5</code>, <code>Error:</code> or an IPv6 address, is searched as plain text, so you can paste a log line fragment straight into the search bar.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bg-blue-500/10 border border-blue-500/20 rounded-lg p-4">
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <Icon name="lucide:info" class="w-5 h-5 text-blue-500 mt-0.5 shrink-0" />
        <div>
          <h4 class="font-medium mb-1">When does <code>key:value</code> count as a field?</h4>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            Only when the key starts with a letter or underscore, contains only letters, digits,
            <code>_</code>, <code>.</code> or <code>-</code>, and is immediately followed by a
            non-empty value that does not start with <code>//</code>. Everything else stays free
            text, which is why <code>10:42:13</code> and <code>http://x</code> need no quoting.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="field-clauses" class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border pb-2">
    Field Clauses
  </h2>

  <div class="mb-8 space-y-4">
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-border text-left">
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Clause</th>
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Operator</th>
            <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Matches</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-muted-foreground">
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>key:value</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">equals</td><td class="py-2">The value, compared as text, is exactly equal.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>-key:value</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">not equals</td><td class="py-2">Anything else, including logs without the key.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>key:(a OR b)</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">in</td><td class="py-2">Equal to any value in the list.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>-key:(a OR b)</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">not in</td><td class="py-2">Equal to none of the values, including logs without the key.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>key:*</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">exists</td><td class="py-2">The key is present.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>-key:*</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">not exists</td><td class="py-2">The key is absent.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>key:*text*</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">contains</td><td class="py-2">The value contains the text, ignoring case.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      Numbers and booleans compare by their text, so <code>status:500</code> and
      <code>ok:false</code> work as you would expect. Arrays and objects have no text form and
      never match an equals, in or contains clause. Excluding a contains match
      (<code>-path:*admin*</code>) is not supported and is reported as an error.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="negation" class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border pb-2">
    Negation and Missing Fields
  </h2>

  <div class="mb-8 space-y-4">
    <p>
      Put <code>-</code> directly in front of a word, phrase or field clause to exclude it. A
      negated clause matches logs that do not have the key at all. For example
      <code>-env:prod</code> returns staging logs and logs that carry no <code>env</code> field. To
      require the key and exclude one value, combine the two clauses:
    </p>

    <Code lang="text" theme="one-dark-pro" code={`env:* -env:prod`} />
  </div>

  <h2 id="or-groups" class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border pb-2">
    OR Groups
  </h2>

  <div class="mb-8 space-y-4">
    <p>
      <code>OR</code> (uppercase) joins the two terms next to it into an any-of group. Groups are
      AND-ed with the rest of the query, so <code>timeout OR refused db</code> means
      <code>(timeout OR refused) AND db</code>. There are no nested parentheses.
    </p>

    <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 ml-2">
      <li>Between free-text terms: <code>timeout OR "connection reset"</code>.</li>
      <li>Between values of one field: <code>env:prod OR env:staging</code> is the same as <code>env:(prod OR staging)</code>.</li>
      <li>OR between clauses of different fields (<code>service:api OR level:error</code>) is an error with a hint. Run two searches, or use a list on a single field.</li>
      <li>OR cannot join excluded terms (<code>-a OR -b</code>).</li>
      <li>Parentheses are only valid as a value list after a key, like <code>key:(a OR b)</code>. Quote text that contains parentheses.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2 id="built-in-fields" class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border pb-2">
    Built-in Fields
  </h2>

  <div class="mb-8 space-y-4">
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-border text-left">
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Key</th>
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Alias</th>
            <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Supported clauses</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-muted-foreground">
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>level</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">-</td><td class="py-2">equals, not equals, in, not in. Values: <code>debug</code>, <code>info</code>, <code>warn</code>, <code>error</code>, <code>critical</code> (any case).</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>service</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">-</td><td class="py-2">equals, not equals, in, not in.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>host</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>hostname</code></td><td class="py-2">equals, not equals, in, not in.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>trace</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>trace_id</code></td><td class="py-2">equals only.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>session</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>session_id</code></td><td class="py-2">equals only.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>span</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>span_id</code></td><td class="py-2">equals only.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      Any other operator on a built-in field (<code>service:*api*</code>, <code>level:*</code>,
      <code>-trace:abc</code>) is a parse error, so the search bar, the API and live tail always
      agree. Each built-in can appear once as a positive filter; use a list
      (<code>service:(api OR worker)</code>) for several values. Key names are case-sensitive.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="metadata-fields" class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border pb-2">
    Metadata Fields
  </h2>

  <div class="mb-8 space-y-4">
    <p>
      Any other key filters on the log's metadata. Nested objects are reached with dots:
      <code>user.id:42</code> finds <code>{`{"user": {"id": 42}}`}</code>. If a log has a metadata
      key that literally contains a dot, such as <code>http.status_code</code>, the exact key wins
      over the nested path. Paths never index into arrays.
    </p>

    <Code
      lang="text"
      theme="one-dark-pro"
      code={`env:(prod OR staging)
user.id:*
-region:eu-west-1
http.status_code:500
path:*admin*`}
    />

    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      The logs explorer discovers metadata keys from recent logs and offers them as autocomplete
      suggestions and facets. Clicking a value in the details panel adds or removes the matching
      clause in the query text, so the query bar is always the full description of the current view.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="time-and-scope" class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border pb-2">
    Time and Scope
  </h2>

  <div class="mb-8 space-y-4">
    <p>
      Time and project are not part of the query text. They are set by the scope bar at the top of
      every explore page and carried in the page URL, so a shared link opens with the same scope:
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-border text-left">
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">URL parameter</th>
            <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Meaning</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-muted-foreground">
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>project</code></td><td class="py-2">Comma-separated project ids. Missing means all projects you can see.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>range</code></td><td class="py-2">A preset: <code>15m</code>, <code>1h</code>, <code>6h</code>, <code>12h</code>, <code>24h</code>, <code>48h</code>, <code>3d</code>, <code>7d</code>, <code>14d</code> or <code>30d</code>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>from</code> &middot; <code>to</code></td><td class="py-2">A custom window as ISO 8601 timestamps, used instead of <code>range</code>.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      Through the API, pass <code>projectId</code>, <code>from</code> and <code>to</code> as query
      parameters next to <code>q</code>. The last used scope is remembered per browser.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="examples" class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border pb-2">
    Examples
  </h2>

  <div class="mb-8 space-y-4">
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-border text-left">
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Query</th>
            <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Finds</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-muted-foreground">
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>level:(error OR critical) service:checkout</code></td><td class="py-2">Errors and criticals from the checkout service.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>"connection refused" -host:web-1</code></td><td class="py-2">The phrase on every host except <code>web-1</code>.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>timeout OR deadline db</code></td><td class="py-2">Logs that mention <code>db</code> and either <code>timeout</code> or <code>deadline</code>.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>user.id:42 -level:debug</code></td><td class="py-2">Everything user 42 did, without debug noise.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>trace:4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736</code></td><td class="py-2">All logs of one trace.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>order_id:* -payment:*</code></td><td class="py-2">Order logs that carry no payment field.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>path:*/admin* -"health check"</code></td><td class="py-2">Admin paths, excluding health check chatter.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="errors" class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border pb-2">
    Errors
  </h2>

  <div class="mb-8 space-y-4">
    <p>
      Only genuinely malformed input is rejected: an unclosed quote or parenthesis, an
      <code>OR</code> without a term on both sides, an <code>OR</code> between different fields, an
      unknown <code>level</code> value, or an operator a built-in field does not support. Each error
      carries the character position of the problem. The UI underlines that token, and the API
      answers HTTP 400. For <code>service:api OR level:error</code> the response is:
    </p>

    <Code
      lang="json"
      theme="one-dark-pro"
      code={`{
  "error": "OR can only join free-text terms, or values of the same field; use key:(a OR b)",
  "position": 12
}`}
    />
  </div>

  <h2 id="api" class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border pb-2">
    API
  </h2>

  <div class="mb-8 space-y-4">
    <p>
      The query goes in the <code>q</code> parameter. It is accepted by the same endpoints, with
      the same authentication as <code>GET /api/v1/logs</code>:
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-border text-left">
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Endpoint</th>
            <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Purpose</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-muted-foreground">
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>GET /api/v1/logs</code></td><td class="py-2">Search logs. <code>limit</code> up to 1,000, cursor pagination.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>GET /api/v1/logs/count</code></td><td class="py-2">Exact count of matching logs.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>GET /api/v1/logs/histogram</code></td><td class="py-2">Volume over time, stacked by level. Optional <code>bucketSeconds</code> (1 to 604,800); chosen automatically when omitted.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>GET /api/v1/logs/facets</code></td><td class="py-2">Top values with counts. <code>fields</code> is a comma list of query keys (default <code>level,service,host</code>), <code>limit</code> defaults to 10 and is at most 50.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>GET /api/v1/logs/fields</code></td><td class="py-2">Metadata keys seen in recent logs. <code>sampleSize</code> 100 to 5,000, default 1,000.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>GET /api/v1/logs/stream</code></td><td class="py-2">Live tail over SSE. The full query is applied on the server.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <Code
      lang="bash"
      theme="one-dark-pro"
      code={`curl -G http://localhost:8080/api/v1/logs \\
  -H "X-API-Key: lp_your_api_key" \\
  --data-urlencode 'projectId=PROJECT_UUID' \\
  --data-urlencode 'from=2026-10-30T08:00:00Z' \\
  --data-urlencode 'to=2026-10-30T09:00:00Z' \\
  --data-urlencode 'q=level:(error OR critical) "connection refused" -host:web-1'`}
    />

    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      <strong>Legacy parameters.</strong> <code>searchMode=substring</code> treats the whole
      <code>q</code> as one phrase, as before. <code>searchMode=fulltext</code> or no
      <code>searchMode</code> parses <code>q</code> with this grammar, so existing callers keep
      working. Alert rules accept the same syntax in their <code>query</code> field (threshold
      rules only); an invalid query is rejected with HTTP 400 and a position.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="limits" class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border pb-2">
    Limits
  </h2>

  <div class="mb-8 space-y-4">
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-border text-left">
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Limit</th>
            <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Value</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-muted-foreground">
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Query length through the API</td><td class="py-2">2,000 characters</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Metadata field clauses per query</td><td class="py-2">10</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Values in one list</td><td class="py-2">50</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Length of one value</td><td class="py-2">1,000 characters</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Length of a field key</td><td class="py-2">200 characters</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Histogram buckets</td><td class="py-2">At most 1,000. A wider range with a small <code>bucketSeconds</code> is rejected with HTTP 400; omit <code>bucketSeconds</code> to let LogTide pick a size.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Facet fields per request</td><td class="py-2">12</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Facet values per field</td><td class="py-2">50</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Metadata keys discovered</td><td class="py-2">500, from the most recent sample of logs</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4">Analytics reads (count, histogram, facets, fields)</td><td class="py-2">30 second time limit on every engine</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      Numeric comparisons (<code>status:&gt;=500</code>) and nested parentheses are not supported.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="storage-engines" class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border pb-2">
    Storage Engines
  </h2>

  <div class="mb-8 space-y-4">
    <p>
      The grammar, the field rules and the handling of missing keys are identical on all three
      engines and pinned by a shared conformance suite. The differences are in how free-text words
      are looked up:
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-border text-left">
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Engine</th>
            <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Words and phrases</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-muted-foreground">
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4">TimescaleDB</td><td class="py-2">Words use the existing full-text index with English stemming, so <code>connections</code> also finds <code>connection</code>. Phrases are case-insensitive substring matches.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4">ClickHouse</td><td class="py-2">Whole-word and case-insensitive substring matching, no stemming.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4">MongoDB</td><td class="py-2">Whole-word and case-insensitive substring matching, no stemming. Requires MongoDB 5.2 or later (7.0 recommended).</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      English stemming on TimescaleDB is the one documented difference. Quoted phrases are never
      stemmed. See <a href="/docs/storage-engines/">Storage Engines</a> for
      engine requirements.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
